As you can see, we’ve moved everything from the guess input prompt onward into a loop. Be
sure to indent the lines inside the loop another four spaces each and run the program again.
The program will now ask for another guess forever, which actually introduces a new problem.
It doesn’t seem like the user can quit!
The user could always interrupt the program by using the keyboard shortcut ctrl - c . But
there’s another way to escape this insatiable monster, as mentioned in the parse discussion in
“Comparing the Guess to the Secret Number”: if the user enters a non-number answer, the
program will crash. We can take advantage of that to allow the user to quit, as shown here:
// --snip--
println!("The secret number is: {secret_number}");
loop {
println!("Please input your guess.");
// --snip--
match guess.cmp(&secret_number) {
Ordering::Less => println!("Too small!"),
Ordering::Greater => println!("Too big!"),
Ordering::Equal => println!("You win!"),
}
}
}
