Summary
This project was a hands-on way to introduce you to many new Rust concepts: let , match ,
functions, the use of external crates, and more. In the next few chapters, you’ll learn about
these concepts in more detail. Chapter 3 covers concepts that most programming languages
have, such as variables, data types, and functions, and shows how to use them in Rust. Chapter
use std::cmp::Ordering;
use std::io;
use rand::Rng;
fn main() {
println!("Guess the number!");
let secret_number = rand::thread_rng().gen_range(1..=100);
loop {
println!("Please input your guess.");
let mut guess = String::new();
io::stdin()
.read_line(&mut guess)
.expect("Failed to read line");
let guess: u32 = match guess.trim().parse() {
Ok(num) => num,
Err(_) => continue,
};
println!("You guessed: {guess}");
match guess.cmp(&secret_number) {
Ordering::Less => println!("Too small!"),
Ordering::Greater => println!("Too big!"),
Ordering::Equal => {
println!("You win!");
break;
}
}
}
}
